# TUNING - this file is yours. Edit it, restart the game, see the difference.
#
# Nothing here needs a new .exe. Every number below is read from this file when
# the game starts, so the loop is: change a number, restart, play a firefight.
#
# Each line is the value the feature shipped with, written out so you can see
# what you are changing rather than having to guess the default.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SHOOTING THROUGH WALLS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A shot that fails to destroy a wall can now carry on through it with less
# power behind it. How much less depends on what the wall is made of.
#
# Two numbers per material:
#
#   cost  how much power the shot spends getting through at all. If the shot
#         has less power than this, it stops dead and nothing comes out the
#         other side. This is the number that decides WHETHER.
#
#   pass  of the power that is left after paying the cost, the share that
#         carries on. 0.65 means two thirds of the remainder. This is the
#         number that decides HOW HARD it hits what it finds.
#
# So a rifle round with 30 power meeting wood pays 10 and passes 0.65 of the
# remaining 20, arriving at 13. The same round meeting concrete pays 55, which
# it does not have, and stops.
#
#   energyCost  what a laser or plasma shot pays instead of cost. Lower than
#         cost against masonry and concrete and the same everywhere else,
#         which is the one place energy weapons are meant to be different.
#         Delete the line and energy pays the same as everything else.
#
# TO MAKE COVER STRONGER: raise cost, or lower pass, or both.
# TO MAKE COVER WEAKER:   lower cost, or raise pass.
# TO TURN IT OFF ENTIRELY: set every cost to 999.
#
# unknown is what an untagged tile uses. Three Joe craft interiors and two
# vanilla farm tilesets are still untagged, so inside the Defiant or the
# helicopter every surface uses this row. It is priced like brick on purpose -
# a tile nobody has classified should behave like a wall, not like a hole.

# THE ONE SWITCH THAT DECIDES WHETHER YOU EVER SEE THIS FEATURE
#
#   throughBroken   false: only a wall that SURVIVES the shot can be shot
#                   through. A wall that breaks gives an opening instead, and
#                   the opening helps your next round rather than this one.
#                   true:  a shot that smashes the wall also carries on
#                   through it, paying the same cost.
#
# It ships false, which is how it was designed. Then I watched it, with the
# same build and only this line changed:
#
#   false   28 shots hit terrain across six battles. ONE of them reached the
#           penetration code at all. Nothing came out the far side of it.
#   true    56 shots hit terrain across three battles. All 56 reached it, 29
#           carried power through the wall, and 15 of those hit a man or an
#           object standing behind it.
#
# At the armour values X-COM gives tiles, a wall nearly always just breaks -
# and a broken wall ends the shot. So false is very close to "off" and true is
# the feature working.
#
# I did not pick for you. Breaking a wall and stopping dead is a defensible
# rule and it is the one the design asked for. But if you try shooting through
# cover and nothing ever happens, this is the line.
#
# Turning it on makes cover much weaker everywhere at once.
#
# To see for yourself: run the game with OXCE_PEN_LOG=1 set and it writes a
# line for every shot that hits terrain, every penetration and every re-trace,
# into user/openxcom.log.

penetration:
  throughBroken: false   # true makes shots carry on through walls they break
  maxWalls: 4            # how many walls one shot may ever cross. A safety rail
                         # before it is a game number - do not set it high.
  materials:
    unknown:  { cost: 30, pass: 0.35 }
    light:    { cost:  2, pass: 0.85 }   # glass, hedges, fences, netting
    wood:     { cost: 10, pass: 0.65 }   # clapboard, planking, doors, furniture
    masonry:  { cost: 30, pass: 0.35, energyCost: 20 }   # brick, adobe, stone
    concrete: { cost: 55, pass: 0.20, energyCost: 37 }   # bunkers, slabs, sandbags
    metal:    { cost: 45, pass: 0.25 }   # plate, blast doors, vehicles


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE COBRA ECONOMY IS TUNED NEXT DOOR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not duplicated here, because two files holding the same number is how they
# end up disagreeing. The economy numbers are in gijoe_cobra_economy.rul, one
# block per site type, and they are read the same way: edit, restart, play.
#
# The two that change the pace most:
#
#   buildCostFunds   what Cobra must have banked before it will build that
#                    type at all. A front company is free the first time,
#                    because otherwise Cobra starts with nothing, can afford
#                    nothing, and never earns anything.
#
#   fundsPerMonth    what the type earns, against upkeepFunds.
#
# As priced today a lone front company nets 110 a month and the cheapest
# thing to build after it costs 300, so Cobra builds once and then goes quiet
# for three or four months while it saves up. That is the economy working,
# not a stall - but if it reads as nothing happening, these are the two
# numbers to pull.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COBRA ARMS ITS MEN OUT OF THE REGION'S STOCK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Materiel is physical. It sits in the region where it was made and does not
# move on its own, which is the whole reason a convoy is worth intercepting.
#
# wellSuppliedMateriel
#     How much a region needs banked before Cobra can arm the men it keeps
#     there properly. At or above it, troops turn out with whatever the
#     campaign month would normally give them. Below it they take the poorest
#     kit their deployment defines - sidearms instead of rifles or heavy
#     weapons - however late in the game it is.
#
#     Set 0 to switch the whole rule off and go back to materiel being a
#     number that goes up and down while nobody is worse off for it.
#
# appliesToAllMissions
#     false: raids on Cobra's own sites only. true: every Cobra battle in a
#     starved region, crash sites and terror sites included.
#
# Both numbers are guesses. A factory makes 80 a month and a training camp
# eats 20, so 50 means one factory keeps roughly one region armed and losing
# the factory disarms it within a month or two.

cobraEconomy:
  wellSuppliedMateriel: 50
  appliesToAllMissions: false
